<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Kuching</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Kuching is the capital of Sarawak, Malaysia, on the Sarawak River in north-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/borneo">Borneo</a>. A small Malay trading settlement under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brunei">Brunei</a>, it became the capital of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/james-brooke">James Brooke</a>'s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-raj">Raj</a> of Sarawak in 1841 and grew under the Brooke rajahs, whose <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/astana">Astana</a> palace and Fort Margherita stand on the river. Japanese forces occupied it from December 1941 to 1945, and it remained the capital of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crown-colony">Crown Colony</a> of Sarawak and of the Malaysian state.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kuching.jpg" alt="Kuching" width="320" height="182" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Mike86 at English Wikipedia · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kuching_Skyline.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Kuching is the capital and most populous city of the state of Sarawak in Malaysia. It stands on the Sarawak River at the south-western tip of Sarawak on the island of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/borneo">Borneo</a>, and the river divides it into a northern and a southern half. The city covers 431 square kilometres and has about half a million people. Its highest point is Mount Santubong, on the Santubong peninsula 35 kilometres north of the centre. The climate is tropical rainforest, and Kuching is the wettest populated area in Malaysia.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Sarawak was part of the Bruneian Empire from the reign of the first <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultan">Sultan</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brunei">Brunei</a>, Sultan Muhammad Shah. Kuching was its third capital, founded in 1827 by the Sultan's representative, Pengiran Indera Mahkota. The earlier capitals were Santubong, founded by Sultan Pengiran Tengah in 1599, and Lidah Tanah, founded by Datu Patinggi Ali in the early 1820s. The founding was spurred by the discovery of antimony ore on the upper Sarawak River in 1824, which was in demand in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/singapore">Singapore</a>. A <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> report mentioned about a hundred <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malays">Malays</a> and three Chinese houses in the area shortly before. Mahkota's rising tax and labour demands were unpopular. The local Malay chiefs moved upriver, allied with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dayak-people">Dayaks</a> and rebelled against Brunei.</p><p>Neither side gained the upper hand until 1840, when the Bruneian prime minister, Pengiran Raja Muda Hashim, offered the governorship of Sarawak to the British adventurer <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/james-brooke">James Brooke</a> in return for his help. In 1841 Kuching became the capital of the Kingdom of Sarawak under Brooke. Most of the Land Dayak rebels were pardoned and became his followers. The name Kuching was already in use by the time Brooke arrived in 1839, and its origin is uncertain: it may come from the Malay word for cat, kucing, or from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kochi">Cochin</a>, an Indian trading port. </p><p>Under Charles Brooke the town received a sanitation system, a hospital, a prison, a fort and a bazaar. The Brooke administration held a centenary celebration in Kuching in 1941. During the Second World War Japanese forces occupied the city, and they set up the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/batu-lintang-camp">Batu Lintang camp</a> nearby for prisoners of war and civilian internees. The town survived the war intact. In 1946 the last Rajah, Sir <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-vyner-brooke">Charles Vyner Brooke</a>, ceded Sarawak to the British Crown, and Kuching remained capital of the colony.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Kuching remained the state capital when Malaysia was formed in 1963 and received city status in 1988. It is the only city in Malaysia run by two local authorities, one for the north and one for the south. Its people include Chinese, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malays">Malays</a>, Iban and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bidayuh">Bidayuh</a>, and the Sarawak Malay dialect is spoken alongside Hokkien and Mandarin. It is a member of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco">UNESCO</a>'s Creative Cities Network for gastronomy.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Kuching” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Japanese forces landed at Miri in Sarawak on 16 December 1941, seeking its oilfields, and took <b>Kuching</b> on 24 December.</blockquote><a href="/state/japanese-occupation-of-british-borneo">Japanese occupation of British Borneo</a></li><li><blockquote>The English adventurer James Brooke helped the Brunei Sultanate put down a revolt of Malays and Bidayuh near <b>Kuching</b> in 1839 to 1841.</blockquote><a href="/state/raj-of-sarawak">Raj of Sarawak</a></li></ul></section>
